module msnd-criminal-local-rules.local-rules

import core.frcrimp45.{ frcrimp45 }

// The Northern District of Mississippi's own criminal periods under the Uniform Local
// Criminal Rules, counted under the criminal rules (FRCrimP 45(a) governs
// "any local rule"). No trigger carries a service method: FRCrimP 45(c)'s
// three added days follow the manner of service, which is not modeled
// here.
//
// Every other criminal deadline is set by order: at the arraignment the
// magistrate judge enters a scheduling and discovery order setting the
// trial, discovery, motion, plea-notice, motion in limine and jury
// instruction dates, and on request the dates for expert summaries under
// FRCrimP 16(a)(1)(G) and 16(b)(1)(C) (U.L.Cr.R. 10.1); every motion is
// filed within the time limits the magistrate judge orders (U.L.Cr.R.
// 47(B)), and a motion filed after that deadline may be denied as untimely
// (U.L.Cr.R. 47(H)). Conditions that are not deadlines stay in notes: a
// motion carries a certificate of conference unless it is of a kind
// U.L.Cr.R. 47(A) exempts; a Rule 17(c) subpoena returnable before trial
// needs the magistrate judge's prior approval (U.L.Cr.R. 17); the
// probation officer's initial sentence recommendation is not disclosed
// unless the sentencing judge orders it (U.L.Cr.R. 32); motions are
// decided without a hearing unless the court orders one (U.L.Cr.R.
// 47(F)); plea agreements and cooperation motions are sealed as U.L.Cr.R.
// 49.1 provides; misdemeanor cases go to the magistrate judges (U.L.Cr.R.
// 58). No response to a motion for post-conviction relief, including a 28
// U.S.C. § 2255 motion, is required unless the court directs one
// (U.L.Cr.R. 47(C)(1)); federal habeas belongs to lit/habeas.
//
// The earlier N.D. Miss. criminal outline's FRCrimP flow and its decisions
// (United States v. Salerno, Brady v. Maryland, Barker v. Wingo, Zedner v.
// United States, Boykin v. Alabama, Missouri v. Frye, Jackson v. Virginia,
// United States v. Booker, Roe v. Flores-Ortega, Garza v. Idaho) are
// FRCrimP, Speedy Trial Act or constitutional authority and belong to
// lit/frcrimp; they are removed here: no reporter text is vendored.

/// A memorandum of authorities supporting a motion, response or rebuttal:
/// at most 35 pages without prior leave of court; it is filed at the same
/// time as the paper it supports (U.L.Cr.R. 47(E)).
limit memorandum {
  pages <= 35 cite "U.L.Cr.R. 47(E)"
} cite "U.L.Cr.R. 47(E)"

/// A motion is filed (U.L.Cr.R. 47(B)).
event MotionFiled() paper memorandum
/// The response to a motion is served (U.L.Cr.R. 47(C), (D)).
event ResponseServed() paper memorandum
/// A rebuttal is filed (U.L.Cr.R. 47(D)).
event RebuttalFiled() paper memorandum

/// U.L.Cr.R. 47(C)(1): within eleven calendar days after a motion is
/// filed, the opposing party responds to it or notifies the court of its
/// intent not to respond. For a motion other than a dispositive motion or
/// a motion for post-conviction relief, the court may grant the motion as
/// unopposed if a party does neither in time.
deadline response-period: 11.days after MotionFiled() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "U.L.Cr.R. 47(C)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}

/// U.L.Cr.R. 47(D): counsel who desires to file a rebuttal may do so
/// within five calendar days after service of the response.
deadline rebuttal-period: 5.days after ResponseServed() counted_by frcrimp45 cite "U.L.Cr.R. 47(D)"
{
  forum frcrimp
}
